const description = 'Frequently asked questions about one rep max calculation: which formula is most accurate, how often to test, training applications, programming off estimated 1RMs, and more.';
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <h1>Frequently Asked Questions</h1>
  <p><em>Reviewed by Marcus Reeve (MR), Editor-in-Chief &amp; Strength Programming Lead. Updated May 2026.</em></p>

  <h2>Which 1RM formula is most accurate?</h2>
  <p>For most trained lifters performing fewer than 10 reps with good technique, all four formulas (Brzycki, Epley, Lombardi, O'Conner) fall within roughly 5–10% of each other and within a similar range of an actual tested 1RM. We average them precisely because no single formula is most accurate across all rep ranges, body types, and lifts. Brzycki tends to be slightly more conservative; Epley slightly more generous; O'Conner most conservative at higher reps. The average of four dampens the bias of any individual formula. See the <a href="/methodology">methodology page</a> for formula derivations and accuracy expectations by rep range.</p>

  <h2>Should I actually test my 1RM?</h2>
  <p>Most lifters don't need to. Estimated 1RMs from submaximal sets are accurate enough for percentage-based programming, and directly testing a true maximum carries real injury risk, especially for lifters who aren't prepared for max-effort lifting technique. The cases where direct testing makes sense: you are competing in powerlifting or another strength sport where attempt selection requires an accurate recent maximum; you are entering a new training phase and want a clean baseline; or you have been using an estimated number for several months and want to verify it against reality. For most recreational and intermediate lifters in ongoing training, recalculating from a heavy training set every 4–8 weeks is sufficient without ever going to a true maximum attempt. See <a href="/guides/how-to-test-1rm-safely">how to test your 1RM safely</a> if you decide to test.</p>

  <h2>How often should I recalculate my 1RM?</h2>
  <p>For an active training program, recalculating every 4–8 weeks based on your heaviest recent submaximal set is reasonable. The practical trigger is a training performance that clearly exceeds what your current estimated 1RM would predict — if you're completing sets that should be at 85% at what feels like 75% effort, your 1RM has likely moved up. Avoid recalculating after a single exceptionally good day — one great training session doesn't establish a new baseline. Most percentage-based programs build in recalculation points at the end of each training block (typically 4–12 weeks), which is the natural rhythm for 1RM updates.</p>

  <h2>Why are the four formulas giving different answers?</h2>
  <p>Each formula was derived from a different dataset using different research populations and a slightly different mathematical model of the relationship between submaximal reps and 1RM. Brzycki and Epley use linear models; Lombardi uses a power curve; O'Conner uses a more conservative linear model. They diverge most significantly at rep counts above 10, where the models' underlying assumptions — calibrated on lower-rep data — start to break down. Below 10 reps the spread is usually small (within 5–10 lbs for most lifters). The averaged result reduces the impact of any single formula's systematic bias for your particular rep range and lifting profile.</p>

  <h2>Does this calculator work for all lifts?</h2>
  <p>The four formulas were calibrated primarily on barbell squat, bench press, and deadlift data from trained athletes. Predictions are most reliable for those lifts. For overhead press, barbell rows, and other compound barbell movements, the formulas generally perform reasonably well with slightly wider error margins. For machine exercises, unilateral movements, and isolation exercises, 1RM estimation from multiple-rep sets is less reliable — the load-to-muscle-group relationship differs substantially from compound barbell movements, and the formulas weren't validated in those contexts. Treat machine and isolation 1RM estimates as rough approximations rather than programming baselines.</p>

  <h2>Why does my calculated 1RM differ from what I can actually lift?</h2>
  <p>Several factors can create a gap between your estimated and actual 1RM. The most common: the submaximal set used for the estimate was performed with accumulated fatigue (making it an underestimate), the set was stopped before true failure (also an underestimate), technique degraded significantly on the final reps making the input inaccurate, or you are a "rep athlete" whose performance scales unusually well at higher reps versus a true single. In the other direction, if your estimate is higher than what you can actually lift, the most common explanations are that the input set used too many reps (above 10), or that you are a "strength athlete" whose performance is disproportionately high on singles relative to multiple-rep sets. Both patterns exist; the formulas assume you're near the middle of that distribution.</p>

  <h2>What is a "training max" and why should I use one?</h2>
  <p>A training max is a conservative version of your estimated 1RM — typically set at 85–90% of your calculated maximum — that you use as the denominator for all percentage-based training. The purpose is to build in a buffer: your calculated 1RM is an estimate with a margin of error, you're not always at your peak daily, and programming to 100% of a potentially-inflated estimate creates a situation where your prescribed heavy sessions are harder than intended and recovery suffers. Programs like Jim Wendler's 5/3/1 explicitly use a training max of 90% of estimated 1RM for exactly this reason. If you find that your program's heavy prescribed sessions (typically 85–95% of training max) consistently feel easier than the rep targets imply, it's safe to bump your training max up by 5–10 lbs.</p>

  <h2>Why is the calculator showing different results on different sites?</h2>
  <p>Different sites use different combinations of formulas and some weight certain formulas more heavily than others. Some sites use only Epley; others use Brzycki; some use three formulas and exclude one. A few use proprietary or undocumented calculation methods. We use a straight unweighted average of four standard formulas (Brzycki, Epley, Lombardi, O'Conner). The differences are typically small — within 5% — for sets under 10 reps, but can become more noticeable at higher rep counts where the formulas diverge more significantly.</p>

  <h2>How does the 1RM calculator connect to the other calculators on the site?</h2>
  <p>The 1RM calculator output feeds directly into the other calculators on this site. Your predicted 1RM is the input for the <a href="/powerlifting-total-calculator">powerlifting total calculator</a> (combining your three lift maxima), and the <a href="/wilks-calculator">Wilks calculator</a> uses your total to produce a bodyweight-normalized score. The <a href="/macro-calculator">macro calculator</a> is independent of your 1RM but complements the programming workflow — nutrition targets are a key input to whether your training percentages are achievable from week to week.</p>

  <h2>Can I use this calculator for Olympic weightlifting?</h2>
  <p>The formulas can be applied to the snatch and clean and jerk, but with important caveats. Olympic lifts are significantly more technique-intensive than the barbell powerlifts, and technique breakdown at higher rep counts is more severe and more dangerous. Additionally, most Olympic lifters train at lower rep counts (1–3 reps) more consistently than powerlifters, which actually makes submaximal 1RM estimation from 1–3 rep sets quite accurate. Apply the formulas to Olympic lifts only on sets performed with technically sound execution, and use the 1–5 rep range for the most reliable estimates.</p>
</BaseLayout>
